  "summary": "Recent preprints test how far simple decoders recover visual information from primate spiking and how RL-trained recurrent networks match primate choices.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how far simple computational models recover perceptual content and behavioral structure from neural activity. In primate intracortical spiking data, a temporal-attention plus MLP decoder reaches 70% top-1 retrieval of visual images, showing that substantial visual information is extractable with a lightweight readout. Separately, recurrent networks trained with deep reinforcement learning on a noisy task reproduce primate-like speed-accuracy tradeoffs and changes of mind, with neural-like dynamics. A published study of mouse visual cortex examines higher-level spatial prediction within predictive processing theory, concerning how spatial and temporal context shape sensory prediction. The new element across these items is the joint treatment of decoding and decision-level computation within trained models, though the evidence base here is limited to three items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-20"
}
